{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-117590",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The constant $D$ is the vertical shift of the function.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14256025/assets/230b2c0476083f8c8faf35e4a762f30e4c43af5fefbdff67b691e58da5a63dd0.png)\n\nTo plot the given graph, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n- Stretch it horizontally by a stretch factor of $2$ to give $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}).$\n\n- Then, shift it by $2$ units up to give $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+2.$\n\nTherefore $D=2.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
